// E0507 — A `Drop::drop` BODY MAY NOT MOVE A FIELD OF `self` OUT.
// ⚠ RENAMED FROM `_e0509` 2026-09-10 WITH THE STDLIB-`Drop` CORPUS CONVERSION,
//   AND THE NEW CODE IS THE RUST-CANONICAL ONE. The subject is unchanged. This
//   file used to declare its own by-value `trait Drop { fn drop(self: Self); }`;
//   it now binds the stdlib lang item, whose receiver is `&mut Self`, so the move
//   is refused for the REFERENCE and never reaches the E0509 arm. That is exactly
//   what rustc does: tests/ui/borrowck/borrowck-describe-field-generic-param-owned-box
//   moves `self.0` inside a `fn drop(&mut self)` body and its `.stderr` reads
//   `error[E0507]: cannot move out of `self.0` which is behind a mutable reference`.
//   E0509 is NOT lost: its Rust-canonical carrier is the move-out-of-a-Drop-type
//   pattern binding (upstream disallowed-deconstructing-destructing-struct-match),
//   and TEN fixtures in this tree still pin that sentence.
// `@rule intrinsic.drop.move-out-of-drop-type-refused`.
//
// This is the FAIL half of a pair. The pass half,
// tests/logos/pass/drop_body_moves_own_field_e0507_ok, is this program ONE
// TOKEN apart — `let q: N = self.inner;` becomes `let q: i64 = self.inner.w;`,
// a Copy read of the same field — and it RUNS, asserting the destructor count.
//
// Was soundness_queue row `drop_body_moving_field_double_drops_local` (tier 1,
// run 1), closed 2026-09-08 by the owner's Rust-canonical drop decision. Before:
// the program compiled and the moved-out field was destroyed TWICE (count 2 for
// a single live value, want 1) — a double free with a green exit code on every
// oracle in the tree except this one. The fix is not "drop it once": under the
// canonical rule the program does not exist, because `Drop::drop` is guaranteed
// the whole value.
package drop_body_moves_own_field_e0507;
extern fn malloc(n: i64) -> *mut u8;

struct N { w: i64, c: *mut i64 }
impl Drop for N { fn drop(self: &mut Self) { unsafe { self.c[0] = self.c[0] + self.w; } } }

struct P2 { inner: N }
impl Drop for P2 { fn drop(self: &mut Self) { let q: N = self.inner; } }

unsafe fn main() -> i32 {
    let c: *mut i64 = malloc(8) as *mut i64;
    c[0] = 0i64;
    { let p: P2 = P2 { inner: N { w: 7i64, c: c } }; }
    if c[0] != 7i64 { return 1i32; }
    return 0i32;
}
